    63	## §2 The AST tuple grammar
    64	
    65	### 2.1 Form
    66	
    67	A node is a vector whose first element is its tag keyword and whose
    68	remaining elements are its slots, in a fixed order with a fixed count per
    69	tag. Child slots hold child nodes inline, so a tree is one nested value:
    70	
    71	```clojure
    72	[:application [:variable +] [[:literal 1] [:variable x]] true]
    73	[:lambda [x] [:application [:variable +] [[:variable x] [:literal 1]] true]]
    74	```
    75	
    76	This is the Erlang abstract-format shape, and it is exactly what the
    77	walker's `case` already dispatches on: every arm of the cold case
    78	(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:359-469`) and the hot case
    79	(`ast_walker.cljc:600-626`) keys on the node's type and reads a fixed set of
    80	named fields. Position replaces name; nothing about the dispatch changes.
    81	
    82	Frame continuations (`:eval-operator`, `:eval-operand`, `:eval-test`, the
    83	stream and FFI frames, `ast_walker.cljc:205-358`) are runtime state, not
    84	syntax. They stay maps, as do closures (`ast_walker.cljc:364-370`) and
    85	reified continuations (`ast_walker.cljc:422-427`). A frame holds the tuple
    86	it is evaluating under `:frame` and reads its slots positionally.
    87	
    88	### 2.2 Slot kinds
    89	
    90	Every slot in the table has one kind. The kind is fixed by the tag and the
    91	position; it is never inferred from the value. A literal whose value happens
    92	to look like a node is a value, which is the rule `linearize/ast-children`
    93	already states (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:230-242`).
    94	
    95	| Kind | Admits | Notes |
    96	|---|---|---|
    97	| `node` | one child tuple | inline in the canonical form (§4) |
    98	| `nodes` | a vector of child tuples, possibly empty | one slot, so arity stays fixed however many operands there are |
    99	| `data` | plain data as `linearize/plain-data?` defines it (`linearize.cljc:45-58`): nil, booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, and collections of them, with plain metadata | never a host function or object |
   100	| `sym` | one symbol | |
   101	| `syms` | a vector of symbols, possibly empty | |
   102	| `kw` | one keyword | |
   103	| `str` | one string | |
   104	| `int` | one non-negative integer | |
   105	| `bool` | exactly `true` or `false` | never nil, never absent |
