   200	## §3 The two boundary calls
   201	
   202	### 3.1 `:vm/store-update` is excluded from the canonical grammar
   203	
   204	The node carries a host function in its `:fn` field and applies it inside
   205	the syntax (`ast_walker.cljc:410-421`). It is already outside every
   206	persistent form: the codec has no arm and throws on it (`v2.cljc:413-478`),
   207	the linearizer lists it as unsupported (`linearize.cljc:19-22`), the
   208	semantic VM has no opcode for it, and no test constructs one (a search of
   209	`test/` for the node type on 2026-09-14 found none). Its one reachable path
   210	is the walker's map-input arm.
   211	
   212	The orchestrator's alternative, a `:fn` slot restricted to a named
   213	primitive symbol resolved and profile-checked per UCF §7.5.2, is not
   214	adopted, because it would add a tag whose whole meaning is already
   215	expressible without one: `(yin/def k (f (vm/store-get k) args…))` is an
   216	`:application` of the `yin/def` primitive (`v2.cljc:128`) whose operand
   217	reads the store through `:vm/store-get`, and the name `f` is then a
   218	`:variable` that UCF §7.5.2 already profile-checks. A second way to say
   219	the same thing would be a second address for one program. The tuple grammar
   220	therefore has no `:vm/store-update`; a tuple loader rejects the tag, and
   221	the walker's arm survives only as long as the map-input path it belongs to
   222	(§9.1), which is a migration matter and not part of the protocol.
   223	
   224	### 3.2 `:stream/close` exists, in both codec and walker
   225	
   226	One grammar truth: the instruction is real. The codec writes it
   227	(`v2.cljc:465-467`) and reads it back (`v2.cljc:549-550`), the linearizer
   228	lowers it (`linearize.cljc:135-136`), `yin.vm.code/mnemonics` admits
   229	`:stream-close` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:12-16`), the semantic VM
   230	decodes it (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:585`) and executes it in its
   231	hot loop (`semantic.cljc:397-399`), and the engine handles the effect
   232	(`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:480-482`). Only the walker lacks the arm
   233	(`ast_walker.cljc:359-469`).
   234	
   235	The walker gains it: evaluate `source`, then raise `{:effect :stream/close
   236	:stream ref}` through `engine/handle-effect`, through a continuation
   237	`:eval-stream-close-source` shaped exactly as `:eval-stream-cursor-source`
   238	(`ast_walker.cljc:319-326`). Removing the instruction instead would delete
   239	working behaviour from the default evaluator to match the non-default one;
   240	the parity suite is the reason the two evaluators share one corpus.
   241	
   242	---
   243	
   244	## §4 Addressing
   245	
   246	### 4.1 The grain
   247	
   248	The address of a tree is `(dao.jing/segment-key tree)` computed over the
   249	whole canonical nested value (`jing.cljc:217-225`). A tree's address is the
   250	merkle grain the protocol pins: every named payoff below works at this
   251	grain and none needs a finer one.
   252	
   253	| Payoff | How it works at the whole-tree grain |
   254	|---|---|
   255	| `:yin.code/derived-from` as a content address | the ledger row `[segment-address :yin.code/derived-from tree-address …]` (§8.3) replaces the entity ref of `v2.cljc:327` and `linearize.cljc:212` |
   256	| the macro ledger | rows `[tree-out :yin.macro/expanded-from tree-in …]` (§8.4): expansion is a function from address to address |
   257	| module manifests pinning AST content | a manifest names the tree addresses it exports; equal content is equal address across hosts |
   258	| lowering as an address-to-address function | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and the pair of addresses is one ledger row (§5.2) |
   259	
   260	The address is deterministic and operand-order-sensitive: `(add 1 2)` and
   261	`(add 2 1)` address differently, because `order-normalize` sorts maps and
   262	sets but leaves vectors in place (`jing.cljc:45-64`). Literal maps and sets
   263	inside a `data` slot hash order-insensitively, which is correct: they are
   264	values.
   265	
   266	### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited, not closed
   267	
   268	`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized `pr-str` (`jing.cljc:67-71`,
   269	`:203-214`) and `order-normalize` coerces every sequential value to a vector
   270	(`jing.cljc:63`). Therefore `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]`
   271	address identically today although they are different values. This is
   272	`dao.jing.md`'s first open item (*Canonical encoding*), inherited unchanged,
   273	and it is an acceptance blocker of this design (§10.1). When the pinned
   274	byte encoding lands, every address minted under this design changes with
   275	every other `dao.jing` address, which is the dependency UCF §7.3.2 already
   276	accepts.
   277	
   278	### 4.3 Per-node addresses and the per-lambda follow-up
   279	
   280	The flat projection (§6.1) keys each node by the address of its own
   281	inlined subtree, computed by the same `segment-key`. These per-node
   282	addresses are a consequence of the hash function applied recursively; they
   283	exist in the projection and nowhere in the canonical object, and nothing in
   284	the protocol names them except queries.
   285	
   286	**Per-lambda subterm addressing is a follow-up, not a blocker.** It means
   287	something stronger than a per-node hash: a `:lambda` whose body is
   288	*materialized separately*, referred to by address from the canonical form
   289	of the enclosing tree, lowered to its own segment, and pinned by manifests
   290	on its own. That changes the canonical form (a `node` slot may hold an
   291	address) and the lowering (one segment per lambda rather than out-of-line
   292	bodies in one segment, `linearize.cljc:151-158`). Nothing in this design
   293	depends on it; when it is designed it adds a slot kind and a lowering rule
   294	and invalidates no address of a tree that does not use it.
   295	
   296	### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`
   297	
   298	Today a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned
   299	entity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda
   300	(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it
   301	(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each
   302	call-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.
   303	
   304	Content addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one
   305	`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one
   306	address by construction, one row in the flat projection, and a query "which
   307	sites reference this lambda" is a join on its address (§6.4). The canonical
   308	object stays fully inlined; sharing is a property of the projection and of
   309	storage, never of identity. `:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the
   310	codec.
   311	
   312	---
   313	
   314	## §5 The segment level
   315	
   316	### 5.1 The instruction vector is UCF §7.3.2
   317	
   318	The segment's canonical form is the positional instruction tuple vector of
   319	UCF §7.3.2: one tuple per instruction, pc is the index, the
   320	`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 table fixes each mnemonic's arity and operand
   321	kinds, defaults are saturated, refs are resolved pcs, and the header folds
   322	away. Its address is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`, the value
   323	`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.2 already types `:yin.code/hash` as. This document
   324	adds nothing to that form and restates none of it.
   325	
   326	### 5.2 The hash chain
   327	
   328	```
   329	tree-address ──lower──▶ segment-address
   330	```
   331	
   332	`lower` is a pure function from one content-addressed value to another. It
   333	is verified, not trusted: a consumer holding a tree recomputes
   334	`(segment-key (lower tree))` and compares it with the segment address the
   335	ledger claims; disagreement is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` (UCF §7.3.4), never a
   336	silent repair. For the comparison to be meaningful, lowering is
   337	deterministic by rule: labels are numbered in emission order
   338	(`linearize.cljc:76-77`), bodies are emitted in the order lambdas are
   339	encountered (`linearize.cljc:151-158`), and evaluation order is the
   340	walker's (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.3). A lowering that introduces any
   341	host-dependent choice breaks the chain and is a defect.
   342	
   343	The chain is recorded as one ledger row, `[segment-address
   344	:yin.code/derived-from tree-address t :derive]` (§8.3). Two trees that lower
   345	to one vector share the segment row; one tree lowered by two contract
   346	revisions (UCF §7.3.3) yields two rows.
   347	
   348	### 5.3 Instruction provenance
   349	
   350	`:yin.code/source` (`v2.cljc:326`, `linearize.cljc:225`) leaves the
   351	instruction: UCF §7.3.2 excludes it from the vector, and the tuple form has
   352	nowhere else to put it. It becomes the side table `[segment-address pc
   353	node-address]` of §2.5, emitted by the lowering beside the vector. Rendering
   354	an instruction range back to source is a join over that table and the flat
   355	projection, which is the query `yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.3 says it is.
   356	
   357	---
   358	
   359	## §6 Querying
   360	
   361	### 6.1 The flat per-node projection
   362	
   363	`project : tree → #{[id tag & slots]}`, one row per distinct subtree:
   364	
   365	- `id` is the subtree's address (§4.3);
   366	- `tag` is the node's tag;
   367	- a `node` slot holds the child's address; a `nodes` slot holds a vector of
   368	  child addresses; every other slot holds its data unchanged.
   369	
   370	Because a tag has one arity in the tree, it has one arity in the projection
   371	(arity plus one for the id). Rows of one tag are homogeneous, so a
   372	where-pattern for a tag has exactly that tag's arity and no padding. Rows
   373	from many trees union into one relation without collision, because ids are
   374	addresses. Identical subtrees collapse to one row; an occurrence count is
   375	a count over parent slots, not over rows.
   376	
   377	The §2.1 example projects to:
   378	
   379	```clojure
   380	#{[A :lambda [x] B]
   381	  [B :application C [D E] true]
   382	  [C :variable +]
   383	  [D :variable x]
   384	  [E :literal 1]}          ; A..E are :segment/sha256-… addresses
   385	```
   386	
   387	### 6.2 Segment rows
   388	
   389	`project-segment : vector → [[pc tag & ops] …]`, the instruction vector with
   390	pc prepended. This relation is per segment: pcs collide across segments,
   391	and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple slot
   392	(`datom.world.md:34`). A query over several segments takes them as
   393	separate sources (`:in $ $2 …`), or uses the union form `[segment-address pc
   394	tag & ops]`, which adds one to every tag's arity uniformly and keeps
   395	per-tag arity fixed.
   396	
   397	### 6.3 `q` over both, and the mechanics it rests on
   398	
   399	`q` accepts these relations through `query/relation`
   400	(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`), whose contents are an arbitrary

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.tuples.md | sed -n '397,800p'" in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld
 succeeded in 0ms:
   397	### 6.3 `q` over both, and the mechanics it rests on
   398	
   399	`q` accepts these relations through `query/relation`
   400	(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`), whose contents are an arbitrary
   401	mixed-dimensional tuple collection. A pattern clause is exact-arity
   402	positional unification: `eval-pattern-clause` (`query.cljc:894-907`) parses
   403	the pattern (`query.cljc:419-436`), and on the general path unifies every
   404	row through `unify-slots` (`query.cljc:799-809`), which first requires
   405	`tuple-shape-matches?` (`query.cljc:439-441`) and then unifies slot by
   406	slot. The 3-slot EAV fast path (`query.cljc:899`) engages only when the
   407	source carries a fact index, which `fact-relation` (`query.cljc:172-177`)
   408	and the datom views supply; the code relations never do, so every code
   409	pattern takes the general path by construction.
   410	
   411	Verified shapes, each of which is a rule a query author may rely on:
   412	
   413	- **Per-tag selection.** `[?id :variable ?name]` matches only arity-3 rows.
   414	- **Joins across arities.** `[?site :application ?op _ _] [?op :variable
   415	  ?f]` joins an arity-5 row to an arity-3 row on the address.
   416	- **Operand membership** goes through a predicate: `[(member? ?operands
   417	  ?x)]` with `member?` supplied under `:fns`.
   418	- **Ledger as-of** (§8.5) and **ref-to-code joins** (§6.4) run in one
   419	  query, the ref pattern on the fast path and the code pattern on the
   420	  general path.
   421	
   422	Two gotchas are rules:
   423	
   424	- `match` (`query.cljc:520-528`) is constant-or-wildcard selection:
   425	  `slots-match?` (`query.cljc:444-449`) treats only `_`, nil, and the
   426	  internal free marker as wildcards (`query.cljc:409-411`), so a `?var`
   427	  written in a `match` pattern is compared as a constant and binds nothing.
   428	  Use `q` to bind.
   429	- `:fns` keys are quoted symbols: `eval-fn-clause` looks the clause head up
   430	  as a symbol (`query.cljc:1099`), and the builtins merge under the same
   431	  keys (`query.cljc:1436-1438`). `{:fns {'member? f}}`, as
   432	  `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:228` writes it.
   433	
   434	### 6.4 Refs joined by address
   435	
   436	A ref is a datom whose `v` is an address (§8). Joining refs to code is a
   437	join on that value:
   438	
   439	```clojure
   440	;; every call site, in code currently named `my.ns/f`, whose operator is `+`
   441	[:find ?site
   442	 :in $refs $code
   443	 :where [$refs ?e :yin/code ?root]            ; 3-slot, fast path
   444	        [$code ?root :lambda _ ?body]         ; general path, from the tree's root row
   445	        [$code ?site :application ?op _ _]
   446	        [$code ?op :variable +]]
   447	```
   448	
   449	The reachability from `?root` to `?site` is a rule over `node`/`nodes`
   450	slots when the query needs it; the example above assumes one tree per
   451	relation.
   452	
   453	### 6.5 What goes to the datom projection instead
   454	
   455	A tree also projects to datoms, `[e a v t m]` with tempids minted in
   456	preorder and one extra fact `[e :yin/address addr]` per node so the
   457	projection joins back to refs. This is the successor of `ast->datoms`
   458	(`v2.cljc:372-482`) and is the *projection path* of §7.2. It exists for
   459	three queries the flat rows deliberately do not serve:
   460	
   461	| Query | Why the datom projection |
   462	|---|---|
   463	| attribute-dimension queries with the attribute unbound, `[?e ?a ?v]` | a flat row has no attribute slot; "every fact about this node" is an EAV question |
   464	| `pull` and `entity-attrs` navigation (`query.cljc:715`, `:1481`) | they navigate entity → attribute → value and reverse attrs |
   465	| scale indexing under covered indexes (`dao.space.index`) | the covered-index publisher and `open-published!` are EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET over datoms (`query.cljc:452-476`) |
   466	
   467	The datom projection never becomes an identity: its `e` values are local,
   468	and the only durable name in it is the `:yin/address` value.
   469	
   470	---
   471	
   472	## §7 The VM around it
   473	
   474	### 7.1 Load paths
   475	
   476	Direct tuple loading is primary. A batch on the program stream is one
   477	canonical tree (AST medium) or one canonical instruction vector (code
   478	medium), and the evaluator's loader takes it as is:
   479	
   480	| Evaluator | Loader input | What loading does |
   481	|---|---|---|
   482	| walker | tree | validate (§7.3), set `:program` and `:control` to the tree; no conversion, the tuple is the node |
   483	| semantic | instruction vector | validate, decode positional operands into the image (the `case` of `semantic.cljc:563-593` reading `(nth tuple i)` instead of attribute maps), store under `:code` with the address as the alias column UCF §7.3.4 requires |
   484	| semantic, AST medium | tree | `lower` (§5.2) then the row above; composed by the composition exactly as `linearize/ast-loader` is today (`linearize.cljc:269-286`, chosen in `src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc:63-68`) |
   485	
   486	Datom-batch loading is the projection path: a batch of `:yin/*` datoms is
   487	decoded to a tree by the successor of `datoms->ast` (`v2.cljc:494-559`)
   488	and then loaded as above; a batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms is decoded to a
   489	vector by UCF §7.3.4's projection rule and then loaded as above. The
   490	projection path exists for media that carry datoms and for the datom
   491	projection of §6.5; it is not a second loader.
   492	
   493	### 7.2 Conformance: both paths, one image
   494	
   495	For every corpus program, loading the tree directly and loading its datom
   496	projection yield the same walker `:program` value; and loading the
   497	instruction vector directly and loading its datom projection yield the same
   498	image. This is UCF §7.3.4's conformance obligation, and it is the VM's own
   499	test of this design: it fails exactly when the tuple grammar and the datom
   500	schema have drifted apart.
   501	
   502	### 7.3 Validation is the tuple grammar
   503	
   504	A tuple loader validates before it decodes, and validation is the §2 table
   505	and nothing else. The checker mirrors `code/well-formed?`
   506	(`code.cljc:155-180`): it returns nil or the first defect `{:rule r :path
   507	p}`, where `p` is the vector path from the root to the offending tuple, and
   508	rules run in order so each may assume the earlier ones held.
   509	
   510	| Rule | Defect when |
   511	|---|---|
   512	| `:tag` | the first element is not a tag of §2.3 |
   513	| `:arity` | the count differs from the tag's arity |
   514	| `:slot-kind` | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not a vector with a tag, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of such, a `data` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`, a `syms` slot with a non-symbol |
   515	| `:saturation` | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil |
   516	
   517	A tree that fails is not loaded, and the error names the path. The
   518	instruction vector's validation is UCF §7.3.4's two checks (hash to the
   519	claimed address, satisfy the stamp's tuple grammar), unchanged.
   520	
   521	### 7.4 Dependency closure is Datalog over the tuples
   522	
   523	UCF §7.6.1 computes `:yin.k/requires` by walking every reachable segment's
   524	instructions for `:var` names, store keys, effect ops, FFI ops, and
   525	`:resume` parked ids, conservatively (every name, not only free ones).
   526	Over the flat projection the walk of one address is four queries:
   527	
   528	```clojure
   529	[:find ?name :where [_ :variable ?name]]
   530	[:find ?key  :where (or [_ :vm/store-get ?key] [_ :vm/store-put ?key _])]
   531	[:find ?op   :where [_ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]
   532	[:find ?tag  :where [_ ?tag & _] [(effect-tag? ?tag)]]   ; :stream/* and :vm/* tags, via :fns
   533	```
   534	
   535	and the same four over segment rows with the §2.4 mnemonics in place of
   536	tags. The fixed point is the loop UCF §7.6.1 already specifies: each
   537	address in `:yin.k/segments` is fetched, projected, and its rows unioned
   538	into the relation; a `:closure` or `:lambda` row contributes no new
   539	address at the whole-tree grain (its body is inline) and one address under
   540	the §4.3 follow-up. Reachability from values, streams, and modules is the
   541	value walk of §7.6.1 and is not code; the code part is exactly these
   542	queries. Because the queries are over data, they answer for a tree that has
   543	never run, which is the property §7.6.1 names.
   544	
   545	### 7.5 The hot loop never queries
   546	
   547	The walker dispatches on `(nth node 0)` and reads slots by position; the
   548	semantic VM runs `(aget code pc)` over an image decoded once at load
   549	(`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.1). Neither touches a relation, a ref, an address
   550	index, or a projection while stepping. Projection and query are load-time
   551	and tooling-time operations. This is the rule that keeps
   552	`yin.vm.semantic.md` §6's cost model intact under this design: queryability
   553	is paid once at load, or by whoever asks, never per step.
   554	
   555	---
   556	
   557	## §8 The ledger and refs
   558	
   559	### 8.1 One relation
   560	
   561	Ruling 6's ledger tuple `[t op address ref]` and ruling 7's tracking datom
   562	`[e a v t m]` are one relation, read in two slot orders:
   563	
   564	| Ledger reading | Datom slot | Holds |
   565	|---|---|---|
   566	| `ref` | `e` | the subject: a name entity for naming rows; a code address for address-to-address rows |
   567	| relation | `a` | which ledger relation the row belongs to |
   568	| `address` | `v` | always a `dao.jing` address (`jing.cljc:228-238` is the admissibility test) |
   569	| `t` | `t` | the ledger's clock: the transaction of the `dao.space` that admitted the row |
   570	| `op` | `m` | the operation, from the open vocabulary of §8.2 |
   571	
   572	The datom order is canonical because it is what a `dao.space` transactor
   573	admits, what `dao.space.index` covers, and what the 3-slot fast path of
   574	`query.cljc:899` serves. The 4-slot order is a declared projection of it in
   575	the sense of `datom.md`'s meta-protocol (`:dim/projection-to`), used by
   576	ledger-only queries that do not care which relation a row belongs to.
   577	
   578	The hash chain is the ledger: a row's `v` is an address, the object at that
   579	address is immutable, and the object's own content contains further
   580	addresses (§5.2). Ledgers interoperate by hash: two `dao.space`s that hold
   581	rows about one address are talking about one object whether or not their
   582	clocks agree.
   583	
   584	### 8.2 The op vocabulary
   585	
   586	`m` is a reserved metadata-entity id (`src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:14-19`,
   587	reserved space 0–15 per `datom.cljc:27-28`). The ledger ops extend that
   588	vocabulary; the three that exist keep their ids, and two are allocated:
   589	
   590	| Op | Reserved ident | Id | Meaning of the row |
   591	|---|---|---|---|
   592	| `:assert` | `:db/assert` | 1 | `e` names `v` under `a`, from `t` |
   593	| `:retract` | `:db/retract` | 0 | `e` no longer names `v` under `a`, from `t` |
   594	| `:derive` | `:db/derived` | 2 | `v` was computed from `e` by the function `a` names; excluded from content hashes as `datom.cljc:19` already states |
   595	| `:expand` | `:yin.ledger/expand` | 3 | `e` is the macro expansion of `v` |
   596	| `:publish` | `:yin.ledger/publish` | 4 | `e` was published as the value at `v` |
   597	
   598	The vocabulary is open: a composition that needs a new op declares a new
   599	reserved ident. Readers dispatch on the op they understand and ignore rows
   600	carrying ops they do not, which is the same rule `dao.lease.md` gives its
   601	readers (UCF §7.7.2).
   602	
   603	### 8.3 Rows of the one relation
   604	
   605	| Fact | Row `[e a v t m]` |
   606	|---|---|
   607	| a name refers to a tree | `[name :yin/code tree-addr t :assert]` |
   608	| the name moves on | `[name :yin/code tree-addr t' :retract]` then `[name :yin/code tree-addr' t' :assert]` |
   609	| a segment was lowered from a tree | `[seg-addr :yin.code/derived-from tree-addr t :derive]` |
   610	| a segment is the address a continuation names | `[k-occurrence :yin.k/segment seg-addr t :assert]` |
   611	| a tree is the expansion of another | `[tree-out :yin.macro/expanded-from tree-in t :expand]` |
   612	| the macro that produced it | `[tree-out :yin.macro/macro lambda-addr t :expand]` |
   613	| a definition is a macro | `[name :yin.macro/definition lambda-addr t :assert]` (replaces `:macro?`, §2.5) |
   614	| a checkpoint occurrence was published | `[occurrence :yin.k/id ucf-addr t :publish]` |
   615	| a module manifest pins a tree | `[manifest-addr :yin.module/exports tree-addr t :assert]` |
   616	
   617	`:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name and changes its value
   618	class from entity ref (`v2.cljc:327`) to address. The macro expansion
   619	ledger is the two `:expand` rows; `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1's expansion event
   620	entity keeps its other attributes (`:yin/error`, `:yin/timestamp`,
   621	`:yin/source-batch`) as ordinary datoms about the event, and its
   622	`:yin/expansion-root` becomes the `:expand` row's `e`. This resolves
   623	`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's cross-media identity problem by construction:
   624	addresses are the same on every medium, so no id-level correspondence
   625	between `program-out` and the log has to be promised or repaired.
   626	
   627	UCF §7.7's occurrence ledger is the `:publish` row plus `dao.lease.md`'s
   628	own facts, which are unchanged: custody is leased on the occurrence, the
   629	grantor's transactor possesses the ledger (UCF §7.7.3), and this design
   630	adds no key to any lease fact. What it adds is that the leased subject's
   631	value is now reachable from the same relation as everything else about
   632	code.
   633	
   634	### 8.4 Refs are the reference layer, and only refs are mutable
   635	
   636	A name is an entity; its history is its `:assert`/`:retract` rows; its
   637	current value is the current-state resolution `dao.space.query` already
   638	performs (`dao.space.query.md`, *Current-state resolution*). Nothing about
   639	a name is in the object it names, so renaming, retracting, and re-pointing
   640	touch the ledger only. This is the git ref: `refs/heads/main → hash`, with
   641	the reflog as history.
   642	
   643	### 8.5 As-of queries are ledger joins
   644	
   645	Current value of a name, fast path:
   646	
   647	```clojure
   648	[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?name :yin/code ?addr]]   ; over a current view
   649	```
   650	
   651	Value of a name as of `?t`, over the raw d5 rows (general path; the
   652	ledger's `t` and `m` are slots like any other):
   653	
   654	```clojure
   655	[:find ?addr
   656	 :in $ ?name ?t
   657	 :where [?name :yin/code ?addr ?t1 1]                 ; :assert, at or before ?t
   658	        [(<= ?t1 ?t)]
   659	        (not-join [?name ?addr ?t]
   660	          [?name :yin/code ?addr ?t2 0]               ; :retract, at or before ?t
   661	          [(<= ?t2 ?t)])]
   662	```
   663	
   664	"What was this segment lowered from, and what did that expand from" is two
   665	joins on addresses:
   666	
   667	```clojure
   668	[:find ?src
   669	 :in $ ?seg
   670	 :where [?seg :yin.code/derived-from ?tree _ 2]
   671	        [?tree :yin.macro/expanded-from ?src _ 3]]
   672	```
   673	
   674	---
   675	
   676	## §9 Migration surface
   677	
   678	### 9.1 The mechanical sweep
   679	
   680	Every site that constructs or reads a map node changes to the positional
   681	form. The semantics do not change; the reads do.
   682	
   683	| Site | Change |
   684	|---|---|
   685	| walker cold case, `ast_walker.cljc:359-469` | `(:type node)` → `(nth node 0)`; field reads → slot reads; add the `:stream/close` arm (§3.2); drop the `:vm/store-update` arm with the map path (§3.1) |
   686	| walker hot case, `ast_walker.cljc:600-626` | same, five arms |
   687	| walker frame reads of the held node, `ast_walker.cljc:209`, `:226-232`, `:243`, `:289` | `(:operands frame)`, `(:consequent frame)`, `(:val frame)` → positional reads of the tuple held under `:frame` |
   688	| linearizer, `linearize.cljc:87-148` and `:230-242` | `lower-node` reads slots from the tree instead of `get-attr` over a datom index; `ast-children` becomes a table lookup of `node`/`nodes` slot positions; `lower` takes a tree and returns a vector, and `lower-ast` goes away |
   689	| codec, `v2.cljc:372-559` | becomes the §6.5 projection pair `tree->datoms` / `datoms->tree`; `:yin/tail?` emitted only from `:application`; `:yin/macro?`, `:yin/root`, `:eid` handling removed; `:yin/address` added |
   690	| `code/well-formed?`, `code.cljc:155-180` | keeps judging datom batches on the projection path; the direct path gets the §7.3 checker for trees and UCF §7.3.4's vector check |
   691	| `semantic/load-image`, `semantic.cljc:524-596` | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; the datom path projects first |
   692	| frontends `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`, `python.cljc`, `php.cljc` | emit tuples; drop `:eid`, `:macro?`, `:phase-policy`, and non-`:application` tail marks at the boundary. Until each frontend is swept, one map-to-tuple encoder at the frontend boundary saturates, strips, and converts, so the evaluators see only tuples from the first day |
   693	
   694	Tests that construct map ASTs, counted by occurrences of a node-type key on
   695	2026-09-14 (`grep -cE ':type :(application|lambda|literal|variable|if)'`):
   696	
