   135	
   136	Entity ids are stream-local. A query interpreter retains the physical source
   137	as context and does not flatten independent histories into one relation.
   138	
   139	Components:
   140	  e: Entity ID. Local handle for evolving identity. Relative offset from zero basis.
   141	     Negative IDs are temporary local IDs (tempids), used during compilation and before commitment.
   142	     Positive IDs are permanent IDs assigned by the authoring stream's writer after a successful transaction.
   143	     Reserved range: 0-15 for system entities (always positive) — markers only, see
   144	     Reserved Entities below for why it is this small.
   145	     User entities start at 16 (positive). While local/uncommitted, they are represented as negative counterparts (e.g., -16).
   146	     Entity ID is a local gauge: a coordinate choice for a specific stream, never a
   147	     portable reference. The stream is the namespace — a stream's identity (its
   148	     kickoff hash, see CONTENT ADDRESSING > Streams) is its namespace. The
   149	     gauge-invariant identity is the content hash; e is only a local cache index, so
   150	     different streams assign different e to the same logical fact.
   151	     e is always the bare local offset. It is never widened, fused with a
   152	     source coordinate, or rewritten. Migration relocates a stream and its
   153	     offsets unchanged.
   154	     Semantic identity uses unique attributes (e.g., :person/email). Uniqueness is
   155	     namespace-scoped: :db/unique enforces uniqueness within a stream, and within the
   156	     assigned namespace once stamped. Cross-namespace correlation uses queries.
   157	  a: Attribute. Namespaced keyword.
   158	  v: Value. Inline canonical primitive (<= 32 bytes) or 32-byte content hash.
   159	     Entity references and compound values appear here as hashes.
   160	     See CANONICAL ENCODING for per-type rules.
   161	     v is the one slot that may hold a *foreign* reference. A reference to an entity in
   162	     another stream is written as a stamped id — the two-element vector [namespace offset]
   163	     (namespace 64-bit, offset 64-bit), mirroring IPv6's network-prefix + interface-id
   164	     split, and written "namespace:offset" by that analogy. It fits well under the 32-byte
   165	     inline threshold. A writer never mints one: it copies a stamped id it observed from a
   166	     read. This is an ordinary value, not a coordinate on the datom itself.
   167	  t: Transaction ID. Monotonic integer, intrinsic and stream-local.
   168	  m: Metadata entity reference (always an integer, never language-level nil).
   169	     Establishes causality across streams (since t is local).
   170	     Used for validity (assert/retract), provenance, access control, and cross-stream references.
   171	     This is a strict superset of Datomic's 5th slot: where Datomic stores a boolean
   172	     `added` (assert vs retract), d5 stores an entity reference whose low reserved ids
   173	     mirror that boolean (0 = retract, 1 = assert) and whose high ids (16+) point at
   174	     reified metadata entities carrying :db/op plus provenance as their own datoms.
   175	     The single source of truth for the reserved ids is dao.datom/reserved; code must
   176	     never compare m against a bare integer literal.
   177	     m is always stream-local, like e. Cross-stream causality is established *through*
   178	     m, not by m being foreign: m names a local reified metadata entity whose own
   179	     datoms carry stamped cross-stream references in their v slots. Every entity-position
   180	     slot (e, m) is stream-local; v is the only slot that may hold a foreign reference.
   181	Sizing:
   182	  Datoms can be variable-size (general case) or fixed-size (typed streams).
   183	  A stream can declare a type that constrains the size of each slot.
   184	  Fixed-size streams enable: cache-efficient layouts, SIMD operations, O(1) indexing.
   185	  Variable-size streams provide flexibility at the cost of offset-table overhead.
   186	  Example typed stream: {:e :int64, :a :keyword, :v :hash, :t :int64, :m :int64}
   187	
   188	Value Constraints:
   189	  v is always small: an inline canonical primitive or a fixed-size content hash.
   190	  Compound values and large blobs are referenced by hash; underlying bytes live in a content-addressed store, fetched lazily.
   205	    Derived datoms are excluded from content hash computation.
   206	  Entities 3-15: unallocated, held for further validity/provenance markers.
   207	  These do not migrate: they have the same meaning everywhere.
   208	  User entities, including all schema, live at 16+ and migrate with data.
   209	
   210	  Why only markers are reserved. Built-in attributes (:db/ident, :db/valueType,
   211	  :db/cardinality), value-type markers, and cardinality/uniqueness values are not in this
   212	  range: they occupy the a and v slots, where they are already namespaced keywords and so
   213	  globally meaningful without any number. A schema entity appearing in e position takes an
   214	  ordinary stream-local id like anything else and is named across streams by its :db/ident.
   215	  Only the m slot needs fixed numbers, because m is an integer read on every validity fold
   216	  and a keyword lookup per datom would be a real cost. Reserving more than the markers would
   217	  put global coordinates in a slot the spec declares a local gauge (see e, above) — the size
   218	  is not the objection, the kind is.
   219	
   220	  What the small range buys. The reserved ceiling sets the floor for e's width. At 0-1024 a
   221	  signed int8 stream is arithmetically impossible; at 0-15 it holds 112 user entities
   222	  (16-127) with tempids in -16..-128, and an int16 stream holds ~32.7k. See Sizing.
   223	
   224	Validity fold (implemented for query db-values):
   225	  Assert/retract is resolved at the index layer by folding m, exactly as Datomic folds
   226	  `added`: storage stays append-only and immutable; "current vs history" is an
   227	  interpretation. `(dao.space.query/current source)` resolves current state
   228	  and projects d5 to d3; `(history source)` exposes exact d5. Fact identity is
   229	  `[e a v]` inside one physical source. Independent sources are never folded
   230	  together before validity resolution. The greatest t wins within each
   231	  identity, and `dao.datom/asserted?` / `retracted?` interpret the winning m
   232	  without bare marker literals.
   233	
   234	Datom-specific principles (d5):
   235	  Content hashes for datoms are computed over [a v] pairs only (not e, t, or m).
   236	  Content hash input is the assert(1) datoms; retract(0) and derived(2) are excluded.
   237	  Content hash datoms themselves use m=2 (:db/derived) and so are excluded from their
   238	  own computation.
   239	  No arbitrary URIs and no variables in storage, enabling efficient EAVT/AEVT indexing.
   240	
   241	Merkle property (d5):
   242	  Entity hash = hash(sorted (a, encode(v)) byte pairs).
   243	  In storage a ref's v is the bare stream-local offset (gauge-dependent); the hasher
   244	  first resolves it to the referent's content hash, so the hash stays gauge-invariant.
   245	  With that resolution v is a content hash
