   406	
   407	`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,
   408	which returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and
   409	dangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is
   410	over a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is
   411	acyclic by construction.
   412	
   413	The application branch expands the **operator before the operands** so that
   414	an operator which rewrites into a macro name hands that macro its operands
   415	*as written*: in `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with `choose` → `defn`, `defn`
   416	must receive `(x 1)` unexpanded so the lambda it builds can shadow `x`.
   417	Expanding operands first would violate outermost-first for exactly the
   418	macros (binders) that depend on it. Recognition is not an expansion — the
   419	re-entry is at the same depth — and a successful re-check goes straight into
   420	the invocation arm, which re-expands at `depth+1`, so a chain of generated
   421	applications consumes depth and cannot rewrite forever at constant depth.
   422	
   423	Properties, each a Phase 1 test:
   424	
   425	- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what
   426	  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:
   427	  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,
   428	  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`
   429	  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro
   430	  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend
   431	  had to decide this before expansion.
   432	- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root
   433	  was visited after its last rewrite, *including* an application whose
   434	  operator was rewritten into a macro name by a child expansion
   435	  (`((choose) x)` where `choose` expands to `m`): the application branch
   436	  re-checks after expanding the operator and before touching the operands.
   437	- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct
   438	  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with
   439	  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not
   440	  accumulate depth.
   441	- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every
   442	  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the
   443	  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST. The shadow set is
   444	  extended *at the lambda node*, so an operator-position lambda's params
   445	  shadow its body and not its sibling operands.
   446	- **Generated macro lambdas are rejected in v1 — wherever they appear.** A
   447	  macro whose output contains a `:macro? true` lambda, as a definition value
   448	  *or* inline in operator position, fails `{:kind :invalid-output :reason
   449	  :generated-macro}`; `valid-ast?` is called with `:allow-macro-lambda?
   450	  false` on expansion output. With admission also rejecting source macro
   451	  lambdas outside definition position, `macro-of`'s inline-lambda arm is
   452	  unreachable and kept only as a defensive check. Macro-defining macros
   453	  and generated inline macros are reserved together (§7); admitting the
   454	  second without the first would need a rule for keeping a generated macro
   455	  body as transformer syntax through re-expansion, which is the same
   456	  problem.

